{{Music of Trinidad and Tobago}}

'''Soca music''', or the "soul of calypso", is a genre of music that originated in [[Trinidad and Tobago]] in the 1970s. It is considered an offshoot of [[Calypso music|calypso]], with influences from [[Afro–Trinidadians and Tobagonians|Afro-Trinidadian]] and [[Indo–Trinidadians and Tobagonians|Indo-Trinidadian]] rhythms.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Birth of Soca |url=https://artsandculture.google.com/story/the-birth-of-soca-notting-hill-carnival/ewUxSVEB2-zBVg?hl=en# |access-date=2024-12-18 |website=Google Arts & Culture |language=en}}</ref> It was created by [[Ras Shorty I]] (or Lord Shorty)<ref>{{Citation |last=Gentle Benjamin |title=G.B.T.V. CultureShare ARCHIVES 1995: RAS SHORTY I "Interview" Seg#1of 2 |date=2010-10-02 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xoYM97IqrNk |access-date=2018-11-23 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151223155510/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xoYM97IqrNk |archive-date=23 December 2015 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Beausoleil |first=Annel |date=2022-08-19 |title=Honouring the Inventor of Soca - Ras Shorty I |url=https://socanews.com/news/honouring-the-inventor-of-soca-ras-shorty-i/ |access-date=2024-12-19 |website=Soca News |language=en-GB}}</ref> in an effort to revive traditional calypso, the popularity of which had been declining among younger generations in Trinidad due to the rise in popularity of [[reggae]] from Jamaica and [[Soul music|soul]] and [[funk]] from the United States. From the 1980s onward, soca has developed into a range of new styles.

